Absolutely. Based on our recent work, we've essentially been building a CPU-friendly AI weather forecasting prototype using ERA5 2-meter temperature data as the first target variable. The goal is to learn the entire end-to-end workflow before scaling to higher resolution data, additional variables, and GPU-based training.

What We Have Built So Far
Phase 1: Data Strategy
Data Source

We selected:

ERA5 reanalysis
2-meter air temperature (t2m)
Global gridded data
6-hourly intervals:
00Z
06Z
12Z
18Z
Initial Simplifications

To make training possible on a CPU VM:

Use only ~10 years of data
Very coarse grid (~5° resolution)
Single atmospheric variable initially
Autoregressive temperature forecasting

This reduces:

Storage
RAM usage
Training time

while still teaching all of the AI weather forecasting concepts.

Phase 2: NetCDF → Zarr Pipeline

We discussed:

ERA5 NetCDF
      ↓
Spatial Downsample (5°)
      ↓
Add Time Features
      ↓
Convert to Zarr
      ↓
PyTorch Dataset


Why?

Weather datasets become huge.

NetCDF works well for storage.

Zarr works far better for:

chunking
parallel access
AI training

This is exactly the direction used by:

WeatherBench
GraphCast
FourCastNet
Pangu Weather
Phase 3: Time Features

We added four cyclical predictors:

Hour of day
sin(hour)
cos(hour)

Day of year
sin(doy)
cos(doy)


These help the network learn:

diurnal cycle
seasonal cycle

without needing to memorize dates.

Result:

Input channels become:

Temperature
Hour Sin
Hour Cos
Day Sin
Day Cos


Total = 5 channels

Phase 4: Training Dataset Design

For every training sample:

Input:

Previous temperature analysis


Forecast target:

+6 hours


Example:

2020-01-01 00Z
     ↓
2020-01-01 06Z


Then later:

2020-01-01 06Z
     ↓
2020-01-01 12Z


Thousands of these pairs are created automatically.

Phase 5: Neural Network

We selected a lightweight CNN.

Conceptually:

Input Grid
     ↓
Conv
     ↓
ReLU
     ↓
Conv
     ↓
ReLU
     ↓
Conv
     ↓
Forecast Grid


Input:

5 channels


Output:

1 channel


Forecast:

Temperature +6 hr


This is essentially the smallest version of a modern numerical-weather AI model.

Phase 6: Loss Function

Training objective:

MSELoss


which minimizes:

(predicted - truth)^2


Training loop:

Read batch
Forward pass
Compute loss
Backprop
Update weights
Repeat

Phase 7: Multi-Step Forecasting

After learning +6h:

Forecast repeatedly:

T0
 ↓
+6
 ↓
+12
 ↓
+18
 ↓
...
 ↓
+72


This is called:

Autoregressive Forecasting

and is exactly how:

GraphCast
FourCastNet
Pangu Weather

perform long forecasts.

Phase 8: NetCDF Forecast Output

We modified prediction output to save:

forecast_2020010100.nc


instead of images.

Output structure:

forecast_hour
latitude
longitude
temperature


Benefits:

Compatible with NCL
Compatible with Python
Compatible with xarray
Compatible with GIS tools
Major Issues We Solved
1. CPU-Only Training

No NVIDIA GPU available.

Solution:

smaller CNN
coarse grid
Zarr chunking
2. Chunking Warning

You encountered:

specified chunks separate the stored chunks


This came from Zarr chunk alignment.

Solution:

Align chunk sizes with the source NetCDF chunks.

Mostly a performance issue, not a correctness issue.

3. Forecast Initialization Bug

We debugged:

init_time


problems in predict.py.

Root cause:

Time coordinate handling and NetCDF metadata assumptions.

We redesigned the prediction script so:

Read initial time
Read grid
Iteratively forecast
Create NetCDF output


more robustly.

What This Model Actually Learns

The model learns:

F(T, location, season, hour)
      ↓
T + 6 hr


Examples:

Heating during daytime
Cooling at night
Seasonal temperature shifts
Land/ocean contrasts
Large-scale advection patterns

without explicitly coding atmospheric physics.

What It Does NOT Learn Yet

Not yet included:

Wind
u10
v10

Pressure
MSLP

Humidity
RH

Geopotential
Z500

Radiation
SW/LW fluxes


Without these variables, the model can only partially understand atmospheric evolution.

This is expected for the first prototype.

